  "query_preview": "(Today through Monday)...\nIssued at 126 AM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nYet again, Big Bubble, No Trouble through the remainder of this \nweek, though low rain chances return for the latter half of the \nweekend into early next week.\n\nDry weather is forecast through at least Saturday due to the \nexpansive influence of strong surface high pressure. Broadly \ncyclonic flow will be in place over the region, though any forcing \nfrom shortwave troughs pivoting through this flow will be displaced \nwell to the north, leaving the primary impact to the area to be mild \ntemperatures and low humidity through the week.\n\nTemperatures will be mild and pleasant, with substantially less \nhumid conditions than experienced in recent weeks. Fluctuations are \nexpected as a dry cold front produces some late work week moderation \nin temps, though each day should still be quite pleasant with cool \nmornings and mild to warm afternoons.\n\nThis leaves the primary weather hazard of concern as fire \nconditions, with afternoon relative humidity values likely to drop \ninto the 30 percent range on many if not most days, and possibly \neven lower as blended guidance often struggles to fully capture the \ndegree of mixing out of dewpoints in the afternoons with plentiful \nsunshine, even in settings with relatively light surface flow. With \nthe possible arrival of increased moisture late in the weekend, \nthese values will improve.\n\nThat said, forestry partner input indicates live fuels are moist \nenough to help slow any fire spread, and overnight relative humidity \nrecovery is helping to mitigate fire danger overall, though \nconditions still require monitoring.\n\nAgain, low rain chances return late Saturday night into early next \nweek as a possible tropical remnants from the western Gulf move \nnorthward and begin to undergo extratropical transition. Uncertainty \nprecludes more than low chances at this time, though this will bear \nwatching as a transitioning tropical cyclone and quick hit of \ntropical moisture could produce some heavy downpours and some \nincreased winds depending upon the track.",
